# How the hub reaches the dev containers — every one the same way, so one block.
#
# Each is reached BY NAME on the compose project's `dev` network, at its in-container sshd
# on 2222, as `dev`, with the hub's own key. Nothing here names a host on the fleet: the
# containers are the only things on that network to resolve.
#
# StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new, and why that is enough here: a container's host key
# lives in its own ssh-host volume and survives recreates, so a key is learnt once and
# then pinned in ~/.hub/known_hosts (a volume, so it survives the hub's recreates too). A
# CHANGED key still refuses. The first contact is on a bridge network nothing outside the
# compose project joins. To forget one after a container's volumes were dropped on
# purpose: ssh-keygen -f ~/.hub/known_hosts -R <container>
#
# BatchMode, because nothing may ever prompt: the hub's message channel to a container has
# no terminal to prompt on, and a prompt there would sit for ever. claude-sessions passes it
# on the command line as well.
#
# ControlMaster: the menu holds one channel per container open while it runs, and every
# action that needs the owner's terminal (`ssh -t <c> … agent exec`) rides it — a round
# trip instead of a handshake, on a Pi. claude-sessions' design § The hub.
Host *
  Port 2222
  User dev
  IdentityFile ~/.hub/id_ed25519
  IdentitiesOnly yes
  UserKnownHostsFile ~/.hub/known_hosts
  StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
  BatchMode yes
  ControlMaster auto
  ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm/%C
  ControlPersist 10m
  ServerAliveInterval 30
  ServerAliveCountMax 3
  ConnectTimeout 10
